Everything that changed since stable 1.0.6.

[b]Competing Exchanges[/b]
[list]
[*]Your Exchange picks its own competitors, as many as it likes. Competition between Exchanges is separate from diplomatic rivalries, and it counts whichever side started it.
[*]Each competitor costs influence maintenance by its Standing, from 25 to 150, like vanilla pacts. Competing costs 10 relations at once, then 1 a month down to Poor. A competition cannot be ended in its first year.
[*]The Leading Exchanges list has a compete button on each Exchange, in the style of vanilla's subsidize button: a silhouette while off, lit while you compete with them. Its tooltip gives the maintenance and the relations cost. A small shield marks Exchanges that compete with you.
[*]Contests are a tug of war settled every 5 years. The bar starts from the two scores, and each action that works pushes it 10% your way, up to 5 pushes a side. It settles in five results: a draw within 20% of the middle, a win that takes trust from the loser, or past 60% a decisive win that takes twice as much plus 10% of the loser's investment pool income for a year.
[*]Five competitor actions, each with a one-year cooldown per target:
[list]
[*]Bear Raid costs 0.5% of GDP. The target's Market Confidence falls by 6 over 6 months, twice that in a Speculative Mania. It fails more often the stronger they are, and on a failure you take the fall instead.
[*]Lure Their Capital adds two months of your pool income to your Outstanding Credit. In return, 10% of their Investment Pool income comes to you for 12 months.
[*]Press Campaign costs 25 influence for 12 months and takes 5% trust from their Exchange. It has a 30% chance of exposure, which costs relations and your own trust.
[*]Hold the Line costs 0.5% of GDP and undoes one of a competitor's pushes against you.
[*]Shore Up costs 0.5% of GDP and halves one competitor's moves against you for 12 months, with a 2-year cooldown per competitor.
[/list]
[*]Actions work against Exchanges you compete with and Exchanges competing with you. They scale with the two Exchanges' sizes: a larger competitor costs more to act against and feels each action less, by up to a third. Tooltips show the numbers for each competitor.
[*]When a competitor acts against you, an event lets you answer in kind, shore up, or keep calm for a little trust. Answer in kind names the action and shows its cost and effect.
[*]AI Exchanges pick competitors they can beat within one Standing, preferring higher Standings for the bigger prize: one competitor, or two from International Standing. Early in a round they drop a competition they cannot win. They act more as a contest nears its end, shore up against whoever hit them, hold the line against your pushes, and save their money once safely ahead.
[*]Capital fleeing a Financial Panic goes first to a competing Exchange.
[*]Bear Raids on your market appear as Competitor Actions in the Market Confidence breakdown.
[/list]

[b]Competition section[/b]
[list]
[*]The Competition section in the Exchanges tab opens with Competitor Actions on Us: each Bear Raid or capital lure running on your market, who did it, what it does and the months left, and the last press campaign or failed raid against you within the year.
[*]Two tables follow, Our Competitors and Competing With Us, with one layout: Country, Contest and Actions.
[*]The Contest bar is drawn like Market Confidence: it slides toward whoever leads, chevrons run toward whoever has pushed more, and the result it would settle at now is framed and named. Hover any of its five sections to see what that result would do to both Exchanges.
[*]Action buttons stay lit while they run on the target or wait out their cooldown. A shield after a competitor's name means they have shored up against you.
[*]New icons for the competitor moves, a painted picture for the competitor event, and a new Speculative Excess alert icon.
[/list]

[b]Exchange score and World Centre[/b]
[list]
[*]The Exchange score now grows with the fourth root of capital instead of the square root, and wealth per head counts within a narrower range. The largest economies no longer win on size alone, so other great powers can compete.
[*]World Centre is now held by the top three Exchanges, not first place alone. A World Centre gets -10% loan interest, +10% influence, 30% smaller losses from foreign panics, and one competitor free of upkeep.
[*]A World Centre now receives twice the capital flight, down from three times.
[/list]

[b]Paths to Renown and specialities[/b]
[list]
[*]Three new charter paths: Futures Pit for a Commodity Exchange, Lender to Nations for a Money Market, and Railway Mania for a Share Market. Each has a painted icon.
[*]Place rewards are trimmed and themed to each path, and every path now has a drawback. Drawbacks grow with your place: x1.25 at third, x1.5 at second and x2 at first.
[*]Each path also pulls levers in other systems as a trade-off: competitor actions, ties, panics, bad debts, the credit ceiling, Blue Chips and bursts.
[*]Home of Great Companies now scores Company Strength: the prosperity of all your companies, divided by 20, so it rarely ties.
[*]Deep Pockets gives less investment pool: +0.06 at first place (+0.045, +0.03 and +0.015 below), down from +0.20. Leading Exchanges had about 1.4 to 1.6 times the investment pool of vanilla.
[*]The Renown and Specialities screen is laid out again with shorter cards, rows and tooltips. Place rewards show on the leader flags, and locked paths show their effects, bars and tallies.
[*]Long speciality names such as Home of Great Companies shrink to fit instead of being cut off.
[/list]

[b]Blue Chips and Company of the Year[/b]
[list]
[*]A company at 70 or more prosperity for 24 months running becomes a blue chip and adds 2 Company Strength. Three months running below 70 lose the status.
[*]Each blue chip, up to 5, cuts the Market Confidence you lose to collapsing booms and foreign panics by 4%, and raises your Exchange's trust by 0.4% a year.
[*]Blue Chip Index: each blue chip, up to 5, gives +0.6% Capitalist investment pool contribution and -1% loan interest.
[*]Each blue chip, up to 5, moves every contest with a competing Exchange 3% your way. A Bear Raid that lands rattles one of the target's blue chips: for a year, one month below 70 loses the status.
[*]Losing a blue chip costs trust and 3 Market Confidence.
[*]A new blue chip brings an event: ring the bell for trust, or issue new shares for 2 months of investment pool income. New shares make the company fragile for 5 years: one month below the mark and it loses the status.
[*]A slipping blue chip brings an event: prop it up for 0.3% of GDP and it keeps the status for 12 months, or let the market decide.
[*]Company of the Year: each year the world's most prosperous blue chip earns its country +2.5% Exchange trust and +5% prestige for a year.
[*]Blue chips show a medal badge on the Companies rows of the Speculation tab, with their progress in the row tooltip. The section header shows the count, and hovering it explains Company Strength and blue chips.
[*]New concepts: Blue Chip and Company Strength.
[/list]

[b]Interface and text[/b]
[list]
[*]Finance tabs are renamed Trading Floor, Speculation, Banking and Exchanges.
[*]Tooltips and text are rewritten in vanilla's style, shorter and with concept icons.
[*]Translations are complete in all 10 languages.
[*]The CMF compatibility patch is no longer pinned to one CMF version.
[/list]

[b]Fixes[/b]
[list]
[*]Fixed a Great Issue or its cooldown sometimes never ending after loading a save, which blocked floating another. Issues and cooldowns now count down by month.
[*]Fixed a script error when hovering the button to stop pursuing an Exchange name.
[*]Fixed revolutionary journal entries not being inherited correctly.
[/list]

[b]Old saves[/b]
[list]
[*]A rival Exchange named in 1.0.6 is now a competitor. Beta.11's, which came from diplomatic rivalries, no longer count. Diplomatic rivalries are not changed.
[/list]
